# Compiled objects and the dependency files that now accompany them. The `.d`
# files are written by `-MMD -MP` as a side effect of every compile and read
# back by the `.sinclude` loop at the end of the Makefile -- one per object, so
# roughly seventy of them appear in the tree root on the first build.
*.o
# Anchored to the tree root, and that anchor is load-bearing. A pattern with no
# slash matches at every level AND matches directory names, so a bare `*.d`
# also matched the `pam.d` DIRECTORY -- silently excluding every new file under
# etc/pam.d/ from the repository. The FreeBSD policy only survived because it
# was already tracked; the Linux policies added later were invisible to `git
# add`, and `make install` would have looked for a file no clone contains.
# The objects these dependency files accompany are built with bare names in the
# tree root (see OBJS), so the root anchor matches exactly what it should.
/*.d

# Linked binaries. Anchored to the root so that only the build outputs match,
# never a same-named file somewhere under src/ or share/.
/hikari
/hikari-topbar
/hikari-unlocker

# Generated headers. wayland-scanner writes these from protocol/*.xml and from
# wayland-protocols' own pkgdatadir; the XML is what belongs in the repository.
/xdg-shell-protocol.h
/wlr-layer-shell-unstable-v1-protocol.h

# Regenerated on every build by the FORCE rule, and never the same twice.
/version.h

# The roff manual page, generated from share/man/man1/hikari.md by pandoc.
# Only the markdown source is tracked -- `make install` and `make dist` both
# build the roff page first, so nothing needs the generated copy committed.
/share/man/man1/hikari.1

# Release tarballs produced by `make dist`, and the staging directory the rule
# builds them from. The staging directory is removed by the recipe itself; this
# entry only covers an interrupted run, which would otherwise leave a tree of
# every distributed file sitting untracked in the checkout.
/hikari-*.tar.gz
/hikari-*/

# Core dumps. Kept out deliberately: they embed the full memory image of a
# running compositor, which on this project means whatever was on screen.
*.core

# Agent process docs: machine-specific development notes, not product code.
# Kept on disk for the AGENTS.md workflow, deliberately not published.
.devdocs/
.claude/

# Editor and language-server state.
#
# The two VS Code files that ARE tracked are shared project tooling -- the
# build tasks and the nested-compositor debug configuration -- so they are
# re-admitted by name. Everything else VS Code writes into this directory is
# per-machine (absolute paths, local toolchain locations) and must not be.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
compile_commands.json
.cache/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store

# Merge and patch residue, which is easy to commit by accident after a rebase.
*.orig
*.rej

# Captured compositor output. hikari and wlroots both log to stderr, and
# HIKARI_LOG redirects that to a file -- see "Logging and diagnostics" in
# README.md.
hikari.log

# Captured runtime logs from redirect typos (e.g. `2>1` instead of `2>&1`)
/1
/2

# An unpacked wlroots source tree, if one is kept alongside for reference when
# checking compositor behaviour against the version actually linked. Globbed
# rather than pinned: the pattern named 0.20.0 while this tree links 0.20.2,
# so the one tree a contributor is most likely to unpack was the one it did
# not cover -- and an accidentally committed wlroots checkout is tens of
# thousands of files.
wlroots-*/
